"""Scene definitions for the SAML Single Sign-On and Signature Wrapping teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='SAML Single Sign-On and Signature Wrapping',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains SAML single sign on, and the signature wrapping attack, in Java. [[slnc 300]] SAML is the security assertion markup language. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] With SAML, a customer signs in at their bank, and the bank sends the shop a signed X M L statement: this is Ana. [[slnc 300]] The shop must trust only the bank's own key, and then read only the part of the document that the signature covers. [[slnc 300]] A valid signature somewhere in the document says nothing about the part the code happens to read. [[slnc 600]] Think of a notary's stamp on one page of a bundle. [[slnc 300]] A clerk who sees a genuine stamp somewhere, and then reads the first page, is fooled by a forged page on top. [[slnc 600]] Our example is an online shop that signs customers in through their bank."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online shop lets customers sign in', 'through their bank.', '', 'The demo builds real SAML-style responses,', "signed with the JDK's XML signature API,", 'and runs three shops against them:', '  trusting, pinned-key, and strict.'],
         narration="Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online shop lets customers sign in through their bank. [[slnc 500]] The demo builds real SAML style responses, and signs them with the J D K's own X M L signature A P I. [[slnc 500]] Then it runs three versions of the shop against genuine, self-signed, edited, wrapped, replayed, and expired responses: one that trusts the key in the message, one that pins the bank's key, and a strict one."),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Identity provider: the bank, which signs', 'Service provider: the shop, which checks', 'Assertion: the signed statement, this is Ana', 'Signature: covers one element, by its ID', 'Wrapping: sign one element, read another'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] The identity provider is the bank: it signs. [[slnc 500]] The service provider is the shop: it checks. [[slnc 500]] The assertion is the signed statement: this is Ana. [[slnc 500]] An X M L signature covers one element, named by its I D. [[slnc 500]] And signature wrapping means the signature covers one element, while the code reads another.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['SAML: Security Assertion Markup Language: signed sign-in statements in XML', 'XML: Extensible Markup Language: nested elements in angle brackets', 'SSO: Single Sign-On: sign in once, at the bank, for many sites', 'IdP: Identity Provider: the bank, which signs the assertion', 'SP: Service Provider: the shop, which checks the assertion', 'ID: identifier: the name of one element, such as a1'],
         narration="A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] SAML is the security assertion markup language: the bank's signed statement about who signed in, written in X M L. [[slnc 400]] X M L is the extensible markup language: text made of nested elements in angle brackets. [[slnc 400]] S S O is single sign on: you sign in once, at the bank, and many sites accept it. [[slnc 400]] I d P is the identity provider. Here, the bank, which signs the assertion. [[slnc 400]] S P is the service provider. Here, the shop, which checks the assertion. [[slnc 400]] I D is an identifier: the name of one element, such as a one."),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=["ACS: Assertion Consumer Service: the shop's address for responses", 'c14n: canonicalization: one exact form of the XML', 'SHA-256: Secure Hash Algorithm, 256 bits: the digest of the assertion', 'RSA: Rivest, Shamir, Adleman: the public-key signature the bank uses', 'URI: Uniform Resource Identifier: here #a1, the element signed', 'DOCTYPE: document type declaration: can define entities; refused'],
         narration="A C S is the assertion consumer service: the shop's address where responses arrive. [[slnc 400]] C fourteen N is short for canonicalization: writing the X M L in one exact form, so the same document always gives the same bytes. [[slnc 400]] S H A two fifty six is the secure hash algorithm with two hundred and fifty six bits. It makes the digest of the assertion. [[slnc 400]] R S A is named after Rivest, Shamir and Adleman. It is the public key signature the bank uses. [[slnc 400]] U R I is a uniform resource identifier. In a signature, hash a one names the element that is signed. [[slnc 400]] A doc type is a document type declaration. It can define entities that read files, so the shop refuses it."),
    dict(key='06-letters-3', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['JDK: Java Development Kit', 'API: Application Programming Interface'],
         narration="J D K is the Java development kit. [[slnc 400]] A P I is an application programming interface: here, the J D K's X M L signature classes."),
    dict(key='07-two-questions', kind='figure', title='A signature answers two questions',
         body='two_questions',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] A signature answers two questions. [[slnc 300]] Whose key made it? [[slnc 300]] And which element does it cover? [[slnc 700]] The table shows three shops. [[slnc 300]] The trusting shop takes the key from the message, so a self-signed response signs the attacker in as admin. [[slnc 500]] The pinned-key shop uses the bank's key, but reads the first assertion in the file, so a wrapped response signs the attacker in too. [[slnc 500]] The strict shop uses the bank's key, and reads only the element the signature covers. [[slnc 300]] Both attacks are refused. [[slnc 600]] That is the core of this video: trust only the bank's key, and read only what that key signed."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='The request, and the signed assertion that comes back',
         body='signin_flow',
         narration="First demo: a sign-in, request and response. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the request. [[slnc 300]] The shop sends Ana's browser to the bank, with a request whose I D is req one. [[slnc 300]] It says where the answer must come back: the shop's assertion consumer address. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the response: the assertion, which is the bank's signed statement. [[slnc 300]] Its I D is a one. [[slnc 300]] The issuer is the bank. [[slnc 300]] The name I D, ana at bank dot example, says who signed in. [[slnc 300]] In response to, req one, says which request it answers. [[slnc 300]] The recipient and the audience say it is for this shop. [[slnc 300]] Not before, and not on or after, give a window of six minutes. [[slnc 300]] And the signature sits inside the assertion, and covers the element with I D a one. [[slnc 700]] Both the trusting provider and the strict one sign Ana in."),
    dict(key='09-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — Signing in through the bank',
         body="ONE. Signing in through the bank.\n  THE REQUEST (the shop sends Ana's browser to the bank):\n    AuthnRequest  ID req-1, from https://shop.example\n    reply to      https://shop.example/acs\n  THE RESPONSE (the bank's signed assertion, what matters):\n    Assertion ID  a1, issued 2026-03-01T10:00:00Z\n    Issuer        https://idp.bank.example  (who says so)\n    NameID        ana@bank.example  (who signed in)\n    InResponseTo  req-1  (answers this request)\n    Recipient     https://shop.example/acs\n    Audience      https://shop.example  (for this shop)\n    NotBefore     2026-03-01T09:59:00Z  = issued - 60 s\n    NotOnOrAfter  2026-03-01T10:05:00Z  = issued + 300 s\n    Signature     inside the assertion, covers #a1",
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The request: I D req one, from the shop. [[slnc 300]] The answer must come back to the shop's slash a c s address. [[slnc 500]] The response, field by field. [[slnc 300]] Not before is the issue time minus sixty seconds. [[slnc 300]] Not on or after is the issue time plus three hundred seconds. [[slnc 300]] The signature covers hash a one."),
    dict(key='10-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — Signing in through the bank',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE RESULT:\n    trusting provider              -> signed in as ana@bank.example\n    strict provider                -> signed in as ana@bank.example',
         narration='The result: both providers sign Ana in. [[slnc 300]] A genuine response is easy. [[slnc 300]] The weaknesses only show when someone edits the document.'),
    dict(key='11-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='How the signature is made, step by step',
         body='digest_steps',
         narration="Second demo: how the bank's signature is made. [[slnc 400]] At the top, before signing: the assertion, as X M L. [[slnc 600]] Step one. The signature will sit inside the assertion, so it leaves itself out. [[slnc 300]] Step two. The assertion is written in one exact form, called canonical X M L. [[slnc 300]] That is six hundred and fourteen bytes. [[slnc 300]] Step three. Those bytes are hashed with S H A two fifty six. [[slnc 300]] That gives the digest value. [[slnc 300]] Step four. The digest value and the I D a one go into a small element called signed info. [[slnc 300]] Step five. The bank signs signed info with its private R S A key. [[slnc 700]] The digest is the same on every run, because the ids and the clock are fixed. [[slnc 300]] The signature value is not, because the demo makes a new key each time. [[slnc 500]] At the bottom, the shop redoes both checks by hand, and they agree with the library."),
    dict(key='12-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — How the bank signs the assertion',
         body='TWO. How the bank signs the assertion.\n  BEFORE SIGNING (the assertion, as XML):\n    <saml:Assertion xmlns:saml="urn:oasis:names:tc:SAML:2.0:as...\n    <saml:Issuer>https://idp.bank.example</saml:Issuer><saml:S...\n    (614 bytes once canonicalized)\n  HOW THE BANK SIGNS IT (step by step):\n    1. drop the Signature element itself (enveloped)\n    2. canonicalize: exclusive XML c14n -> 614 bytes\n    3. DigestValue = base64(SHA-256(those bytes))\n       Wx5yAVx5mk5fBilLz82ytRLo9aaIj/66yEPlHDlh7zM=\n    4. SignedInfo = Reference #a1 + DigestValue -> 640 bytes\n    5. SignatureValue = RSA-SHA256(bank\'s private key, SignedInfo)\n       256 bytes = <random: a new RSA key on every run>',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The assertion, before signing, starts like this. [[slnc 300]] Six hundred and fourteen bytes, once canonical. [[slnc 400]] Then the five steps. [[slnc 300]] The digest value, in base sixty four. [[slnc 300]] Signed info, six hundred and forty bytes. [[slnc 300]] And the signature value: two hundred and fifty six bytes, shown as a placeholder.'),
    dict(key='13-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — How the bank signs the assertion',
         body="TWO. (continued)\n  AFTER SIGNING (inside the assertion, after the Issuer):\n    <ds:Signature> Reference URI #a1, DigestValue, SignatureValue,\n                   KeyInfo: the signer's RSA public key\n  HOW THE SHOP CHECKS IT (redone by hand, with the bank's key):\n    1. SHA-256 of the same bytes again = DigestValue: true\n    2. RSA verify of SignedInfo, the bank's public key: true\n    3. the library's XMLSignature.validate agrees: true\n  THE RESULT:\n    the signature covers #a1 and nothing else",
         narration="After signing, the signature sits inside the assertion. [[slnc 300]] It carries the reference, the digest, the value, and the signer's public key. [[slnc 500]] The shop checks it by hand. [[slnc 300]] The hash of the same bytes equals the digest value. [[slnc 300]] R S A verification with the bank's key is true. [[slnc 300]] And the library agrees. [[slnc 400]] The signature covers a one, and nothing else."),
    dict(key='14-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='Whose key made the signature?',
         body='whose_key',
         narration="Third demo: whose key made the signature? [[slnc 400]] At the top, the input. [[slnc 300]] The attacker builds the same X M L, names the admin, and signs it with their own key. [[slnc 300]] The public half of that key travels inside the message, in key info. [[slnc 700]] On the left, before the fix. [[slnc 300]] The trusting shop checks the signature with the key the message carries. [[slnc 300]] Of course it is valid. [[slnc 300]] The attacker is signed in as the admin. [[slnc 600]] On the right, after the fix. [[slnc 300]] The shop checks with the bank's key, which it was given in advance. [[slnc 300]] Not valid: refused. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, a name edited in a genuine response. [[slnc 300]] The digest the bank signed and the digest of the edited assertion are different. [[slnc 300]] So any edit breaks the signature."),
    dict(key='15-act-3-1', kind='console', title="Act Three — A valid signature is not the same as the bank's signature",
         body="THREE. A valid signature is not the same as the bank's signature.\n  THE ATTACKER'S RESPONSE (same XML, the attacker's own key):\n    NameID   admin@bank.example\n    KeyInfo  the attacker's public key, inside the message\n  BEFORE THE FIX (trusting provider: the key in KeyInfo):\n    signature valid with the key it carries: true\n    -> signed in as admin@bank.example\n    <- ANYONE CAN SIGN\n  AFTER THE FIX (pinned-key provider: the bank's key only):\n    signature valid with the bank's key: false\n    -> refused: signature is not valid",
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The attacker's response names the admin, and carries the attacker's key. [[slnc 400]] Before the fix: valid with the key it carries, and signed in as admin. [[slnc 300]] Anyone can sign. [[slnc 400]] After the fix: not valid with the bank's key. [[slnc 300]] Refused."),
    dict(key='16-act-3-2', kind='console', title="Act Three — A valid signature is not the same as the bank's signature",
         body='THREE. (continued)\n  ONE NAME EDITED IN THE GENUINE RESPONSE (the input):\n    NameID ana@bank.example -> admin@bank.example\n  THE DIGEST, AS SIGNED AND AFTER THE EDIT:\n    DigestValue  Wx5yAVx5mk5fBilLz82ytRLo9aaIj/66yEPlHDlh7zM=\n    SHA-256 now  lwyut96xNH6vh7kdE/FybSbFShbhJ04L/DTzEFb+8tM=\n  THE RESULT:\n    -> refused: signature is not valid',
         narration='Now a genuine response, with one name edited. [[slnc 400]] The digest value as signed, and the hash of the edited assertion. [[slnc 300]] They differ. [[slnc 300]] Refused: the signature is not valid.'),
    dict(key='17-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='The signature covers one element; the code reads another',
         body='wrapping_tree',
         narration="Fourth demo: signature wrapping. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the input: the wrapped response. [[slnc 300]] The attacker keeps the bank's signed assertion, a one, exactly as it was, and tucks it into an extensions element. [[slnc 300]] Then an unsigned copy, forged one, naming the admin, goes first. [[slnc 700]] Before the fix, the pinned-key shop asks two separate questions. [[slnc 300]] Is the first signature valid, with the bank's key? [[slnc 300]] Yes: it covers a one. [[slnc 300]] Then, what does the first assertion say? [[slnc 300]] It reads forged one, and signs in the admin. [[slnc 700]] After the fix, the strict shop requires exactly one assertion, with the signature inside it, and reads only the element the signature covers. [[slnc 300]] There are two assertions, so it refuses."),
    dict(key='18-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — Signature wrapping: one assertion checked, another read',
         body="FOUR. Signature wrapping: one assertion checked, another read.\n  THE WRAPPED RESPONSE (the attacker moves the signed one):\n    samlp:Response\n      saml:Assertion ID forged-1, NameID admin@bank.example, unsigned\n      samlp:Extensions\n        saml:Assertion ID a1, NameID ana@bank.example\n          ds:Signature covers #a1\n  BEFORE THE FIX (pinned-key provider):\n    1. first signature, the bank's key: valid true, covers #a1\n    2. first assertion read: ID forged-1, NameID admin@bank.example\n    -> signed in as admin@bank.example\n    <- CHECKED ONE ELEMENT, READ ANOTHER",
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The wrapped response: forged one first, unsigned. [[slnc 300]] Then the extensions element, with a one and its signature inside. [[slnc 500]] Before the fix: the first signature is valid, and covers a one. [[slnc 300]] The first assertion read is forged one. [[slnc 300]] Signed in as admin. [[slnc 300]] Checked one element, read another.'),
    dict(key='19-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — Signature wrapping: one assertion checked, another read',
         body='FOUR. (continued)\n  AFTER THE FIX (strict provider: read only what is signed):\n    assertions in the document: 2\n    -> refused: exactly one assertion is required\n  THE STRICT PROVIDER AND THE OTHER TWO ATTACKS:\n    self-signed                    -> refused: signature is not valid\n    edited name                    -> refused: signature is not valid',
         narration='After the fix: two assertions in the document. [[slnc 300]] Refused. [[slnc 400]] And the strict provider refuses the other two attacks as well: the self-signed response and the edited one.'),
    dict(key='20-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='A genuine signature, and the statement still checked',
         body='time_window',
         narration="Fifth demo: the statement itself is checked. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the time window. [[slnc 300]] The assertion is valid from not before, nine fifty nine, to not on or after, ten oh five. [[slnc 300]] The shop allows sixty seconds of clock difference on each side. [[slnc 300]] Now is ten o'clock: inside. [[slnc 300]] An assertion that ran out at nine ten is far outside. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, one thing changed at a time. [[slnc 300]] The same response a second time. [[slnc 300]] An expired one. [[slnc 300]] One meant for another shop. [[slnc 300]] One that answers a request this browser never made. [[slnc 300]] And a document with a doc type. [[slnc 500]] Each one has a genuine signature, and each one is refused."),
    dict(key='21-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — The statement itself is checked',
         body="FIVE. The statement itself is checked.\n  THE STRICT PROVIDER'S CHECKS (now 2026-03-01T10:00:00Z):\n    issuer   https://idp.bank.example: true\n    time     NotBefore - 60 s <= now < NotOnOrAfter + 60 s: true\n    audience https://shop.example: true\n    request  InResponseTo req-1 is the one this browser made: true\n    replay   assertion id a2 not seen before: true\n  THE RESULT:\n    genuine response, first time   -> signed in as ana@bank.example",
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The strict provider's checks on a fresh response, a two. [[slnc 300]] The issuer, the time window, the audience, the request, and replay. [[slnc 300]] All true. [[slnc 300]] Ana is signed in."),
    dict(key='22-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — The statement itself is checked',
         body='FIVE. (continued)\n  ONE THING CHANGED AT A TIME (the input, then the result):\n    the same response again (assertion a2)\n      -> refused: assertion already used\n    NotOnOrAfter 2026-03-01T09:10:00Z (50 minutes ago)\n      -> refused: assertion is outside its validity period\n    Audience https://other.example (another shop)\n      -> refused: assertion is for another audience\n    InResponseTo req-999 (a request this browser never made)\n      -> refused: assertion answers another request\n    <!DOCTYPE r [<!ENTITY x SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd">]>\n      -> refused: not well-formed XML or has a DOCTYPE',
         narration="Now one thing changed at a time. [[slnc 400]] The same response again: already used. [[slnc 300]] Not on or after, fifty minutes ago: outside its validity period. [[slnc 300]] Another shop's audience: refused. [[slnc 300]] A request this browser never made: refused. [[slnc 300]] And a doc type with an external entity: refused before it is read."),
    dict(key='23-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['XML signatures are hard to use correctly', 'A pinned key needs a rotation plan', 'Clocks must roughly agree', 'Replay protection needs state', 'Encryption, logout and attributes are more work'],
         narration="Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] X M L signatures are hard to use correctly, which is why a maintained library is the right choice. [[slnc 500]] Pinning the bank's key needs a plan for when the bank changes it. [[slnc 500]] The clocks of the bank and the shop must roughly agree. [[slnc 500]] Refusing a replayed assertion means remembering every id for a while. [[slnc 300]] And encrypted assertions, logout, and attribute mapping are more work again."),
    dict(key='24-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['Signing in at work through another site', 'A SAMLResponse field in the network tab', 'Advisories about "XML signature wrapping"', 'A KeyInfo element inside a signature'],
         narration="You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] Signing in to a work tool through another site, and coming back, is often SAML. [[slnc 500]] A SAML response field, in the browser's network tab, is the signed statement on its way. [[slnc 500]] Security advisories about X M L signature wrapping are the fourth demo, in real products. [[slnc 500]] And a key info element inside a signature is the key the trusting shop wrongly believed."),
    dict(key='25-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Use a maintained SAML library', "Pin the bank's key; ignore the one in the message", 'Exactly one assertion, signed itself', 'Read only the element the signature covers', 'Check expiry, audience, request; refuse replay', 'Refuse any DOCTYPE'],
         narration="So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Use a maintained SAML library, and keep it patched. [[slnc 300]] Pin the bank's key, and ignore any key in the message. [[slnc 500]] Require exactly one assertion, with its own signature. [[slnc 300]] Read only the element that the signature covers. [[slnc 500]] Check the expiry, the audience, and the request it answers, and refuse a replay. [[slnc 300]] And refuse any doc type, before the document is read."),
    dict(key='26-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['Idp: the bank: signs the assertion, puts its key in KeyInfo', 'SignatureSteps: a teaching class: the digest and the RSA check, by hand', 'SignatureCheck: validates one XML signature; who supplies the key is the difference', "PinnedKeyServiceProvider: pins the bank's key, but reads the first assertion", 'StrictServiceProvider: reads only what the signature covers, then checks the statement', 'Attacks: the forgeries, made the way an attacker would'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Everything runs in one Java process, with a fixed clock and fixed assertion ids, so every run prints the same text. [[slnc 300]] Here are the classes that matter.'),
    dict(key='27-class-1', kind='code', title='Idp — an enveloped signature over #ID',
         body='// Idp: the bank: signs the assertion, puts its key in KeyInfo\nString uri = "#" + assertion.getAttribute("ID");\nDigestMethod sha256 = f.newDigestMethod(DigestMethod.SHA256, null);\n...\nReference ref = f.newReference(uri, sha256, transforms, null, null);\n...\nKeyInfo ki = kif.newKeyInfo(List.of(kif.newKeyValue(signer.getPublic())));\n...\nf.newXMLSignature(si, ki).sign(ctx);',
         narration='The identity provider plays the bank. [[slnc 300]] Its reference points at the assertion by its I D, with an S H A two fifty six digest. [[slnc 300]] It puts its public key into key info, as many identity providers do, and signs.'),
    dict(key='28-class-2', kind='code', title='SignatureSteps — the digest, then the signature',
         body='// SignatureSteps: a teaching class: the digest and the RSA check, by hand\nbyte[] canonical = readAll(ref.getDigestInputStream());\nMessageDigest sha256 = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");\nString byHand = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(sha256.digest(canonical));\n...\nSignature rsa = Signature.getInstance("SHA256withRSA");\nrsa.initVerify(key);\nrsa.update(signedInfo);\nboolean signatureByHand = rsa.verify(value);',
         narration="This class redoes the second demo by hand. [[slnc 300]] It hashes the canonical bytes of the assertion, and verifies signed info with R S A. [[slnc 300]] A test proves it gives exactly the library's verdict."),
    dict(key='29-class-3', kind='code', title='SignatureCheck — the key from the message, or the pinned key',
         body='// SignatureCheck: validates one XML signature; who supplies the key is the difference\nif (o instanceof KeyValue kv) {\n...\nstatic KeySelector pinned(PublicKey trusted) {\n    return KeySelector.singletonKeySelector(trusted);\n}',
         narration="The embedded key selector takes the key out of the message: that is the trusting shop. [[slnc 300]] The pinned selector only ever returns the bank's key, which the shop was given in advance."),
    dict(key='30-class-4', kind='code', title='PinnedKeyServiceProvider — two questions, two different elements',
         body='// PinnedKeyServiceProvider: pins the bank\'s key, but reads the first assertion\nSignatureCheck.validate(doc, (Element) sigs.get(0), pinned(idpKey));\n// the FIRST assertion in the document, signed or not\nElement assertion = (Element) Xml.byName(doc, Xml.SAML, "Assertion").item(0);\nreturn Xml.text(assertion, Xml.SAML, "NameID");',
         narration="This is the wrapping bug. [[slnc 300]] The first signature is validated with the bank's key. [[slnc 300]] Then the first assertion in the document is read, whether or not that signature covers it."),
    dict(key='31-class-5', kind='code', title='StrictServiceProvider — one assertion, the one that was signed',
         body='// StrictServiceProvider: reads only what the signature covers, then checks the statement\nif (assertions.getLength() != 1) {\n    throw new SamlRefused("exactly one assertion is required");\n}\n...\nElement signature = (Element) sigs.get(0);\nSignatureCheck.Result checked = SignatureCheck.validate(doc, signature, pinned(idpKey));\nString id = assertion.getAttribute("ID");\nif (id.isEmpty() || !id.equals(checked.coveredId()) || countIds(doc, id) != 1) {',
         narration='The strict provider requires exactly one assertion. [[slnc 300]] It validates with the pinned key. [[slnc 300]] And the element it reads must be the very element the signature covers, with an I D that appears only once.'),
    dict(key='32-class-6', kind='code', title='StrictServiceProvider — then the statement itself',
         body='// StrictServiceProvider: reads only what the signature covers, then checks the statement\nif (!AUDIENCE.equals(Xml.text(conditions, Xml.SAML, "Audience"))) {\n...\nif (!pendingRequestId.equals(confirmation.getAttribute("InResponseTo"))) {\n...\nif (!usedAssertions.add(id)) {',
         narration='Then the statement. [[slnc 300]] The audience must be this shop. [[slnc 300]] It must answer the request this browser made. [[slnc 300]] And an assertion I D is accepted only once.'),
    dict(key='33-class-7', kind='code', title='Attacks — signature wrapping',
         body='// Attacks: the forgeries, made the way an attacker would\nforged.setAttribute("ID", "forged-1");\nforged.getElementsByTagNameNS(Xml.SAML, "NameID").item(0).setTextContent(forgedName);\n...\nextensions.appendChild(signed);\nresponse0.insertBefore(forged, response0.getFirstChild());',
         narration='The wrapping attack copies the assertion, removes its signature, and changes the name. [[slnc 300]] The genuine assertion moves into extensions, and the forged copy goes first.'),
    dict(key='34-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Put the forged assertion after', 'the genuine one, and see which shops read it.'],
         narration="That's SAML single sign on and signature wrapping. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] A valid signature is not the same as the statement you believed, so trust only the bank's key, and read only what that key signed. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine, with nothing installed except a Java development kit. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Put the forged assertion after the genuine one, and see which shops read it. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

